/**
 * Returns a user's display name for the UI.
 * Falls back gracefully when preferred/first/last names are missing,
 * finally falling back to email or a generic placeholder.
 *
 * @param {Object} user
 * @param {string} [user.preferredName]
 * @param {string} [user.firstName]
 * @param {string} [user.lastName]
 * @param {string} [user.email]
 * @returns {string}
 */
function formatName(user) {
  if (!user || typeof user !== 'object') {
    return 'Unknown User';
  }

  const clean = (value) => (typeof value === 'string' ? value.trim() : '');

  const preferred = clean(user.preferredName);
  if (preferred) {
    return preferred;
  }

  const first = clean(user.firstName);
  const last = clean(user.lastName);
  const fullName = [first, last].filter(Boolean).join(' ');
  if (fullName) {
    return fullName;
  }

  const email = clean(user.email);
  if (email) {
    return email.split('@')[0];
  }

  return 'Unknown User';
}

module.exports = { formatName };
```

**Notes on the design:**
- Priority order: `preferredName` (explicit user choice) → `firstName + lastName` → local-part of `email` → `"Unknown User"` fallback.
- Trims whitespace and guards against `null`/`undefined`/non-string fields so a partially-populated user object (e.g., only a `lastName`) doesn't produce `" Smith"` or `"undefined undefined"`.
- Guards against `user` itself being missing, since display-name formatting is often called in contexts (loading states, deleted accounts) where the full user record isn't available yet.
- If your UI needs initials (e.g., for an avatar) rather than a full name, that's a distinct function — happy to add a `getInitials(user)` alongside this if useful.

